==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|biorn}}

===Etymology===
{{root|ang|ine-pro|*bʰerH-|id=brown|*bʰerH-|id2=pierce|*ǵʰwer-}}
The origin is disputed. A phonetically exact correspondence exists in {{cog|non|bjǫrn||bear}}, from {{der|ang|gem-pro|*bernuz}} (more at {{m|gem-pro|*berô}}), but the English word is never used for "bear", and the Old Norse word is never used for "warrior". While it is not implausible that the term for a wild animal could become a poetic term for a warrior, Germanic scholars have been reluctant to accept any relationship between the two. Some consider it to be a variant of {{noncog|ang|bearn|t=child, offspring, son", hence "boy, servant}}, while others derive it from a {{cog|gem-pro|*beron-|t=carrier}} (hence "servant; man, warrior", more at {{m|en|baron}}). {{der|ang|cel|-}} origin has also been considered; thus, [[:w:John Rhys|Rhys]] took this to be a Germanic reflex of the Celtic title ''[[Brennus]]'', and [[:w:Henry Bradley|Bradley]] connected British ''[[Bernicia]]'' ({{cog|cy|brenhin}}, {{m|cy|brenin}}).

The word has the form ''biorn'' in early Old English attestations; it survives into early Middle English as ''beorn'' and takes the variant spellings ''bern, berne, burn, burne, bearn, bieren, beern, beerne'' in later Middle English. Middle English usage often interchanges it with [[baron]]. As ''berne'', it survives into the 16th century in Scottish dialect, but becomes indistinguishable from [[bairn]].

